# Code Style Rules

These rules apply to ALL code in this repo — components, MCP server, scripts, docs site. They are non-negotiable.

## No emojis in code

Never use emojis anywhere in:
- Component source code (`src/components/`, `src/mcp/`, `src/theme/`, etc.)
- TypeScript types or interfaces
- Variable names, function names, file names
- JSDoc comments
- Code comments (inline `//` or block `/* */`)
- Console logs (use plain text)
- Error messages
- Commit messages
- Pull request titles/descriptions

Emojis are acceptable ONLY in:
- README.md (sparingly, for visual section headers)
- Marketing-facing documentation
- Example data shown in MCP responses (status indicators in user-facing strings)

```tsx
// ❌ Wrong
console.log('✅ Generated data files');
const isLoading = true; // 🔄 loading state

// ✅ Right
console.log('Generated data files');
const isLoading = true; // Loading state
```

## TypeScript

### Strict mode is mandatory

`tsconfig.json` must have `"strict": true`. Do not disable strict checks.

### No `any` type

```tsx
// ❌ Wrong
function process(data: any) { /* ... */ }

// ✅ Right
function process(data: unknown) { /* ... */ }
function process<T>(data: T) { /* ... */ }
```

If you absolutely need `any`, use `unknown` and narrow with type guards. If you truly cannot type something, leave a comment explaining why.

### Prefer interfaces for object shapes, types for unions

```tsx
// Object shapes → interface
interface ButtonProps {
  variant?: ButtonVariant;
  size?: ButtonSize;
}

// Unions, intersections, primitives → type
type ButtonVariant = 'primary' | 'secondary' | 'outline' | 'ghost' | 'destructive';
type ButtonSize = 'sm' | 'md' | 'lg';
```

### Explicit return types on exported functions

```tsx
// ❌ Wrong
export function getComponent(name: string) { /* ... */ }

// ✅ Right
export function getComponent(name: string): ComponentData | null { /* ... */ }
```

### No `enum` — use const objects

```tsx
// ❌ Wrong
enum Status { Active, Inactive, Pending }

// ✅ Right
export const Status = {
  Active: 'active',
  Inactive: 'inactive',
  Pending: 'pending',
} as const;
export type Status = (typeof Status)[keyof typeof Status];
```

## Naming conventions

| What | Convention | Example |
|------|-----------|---------|
| Components | PascalCase | `Button`, `MetricCard` |
| Component files | PascalCase | `Button.tsx` |
| Hooks | camelCase, `use` prefix | `useTheme`, `useTokens` |
| Utility functions | camelCase | `formatCurrency`, `getComponent` |
| Constants | UPPER_SNAKE_CASE | `MAX_RETRIES`, `DEFAULT_SIZE` |
| Types/Interfaces | PascalCase | `ButtonProps`, `TokenData` |
| Boolean variables | `is`/`has`/`should` prefix | `isLoading`, `hasError`, `shouldRender` |
| Event handlers | `handle` prefix | `handleClick`, `handleChange` |
| Event prop names | `on` prefix | `onClick`, `onChange` |

## Imports

### Order matters

```tsx
// 1. React / Next.js
import * as React from 'react';
import { useRouter } from 'next/navigation';

// 2. External packages
import { Button as MuiButton } from '@mui/material';
import { z } from 'zod';

// 3. Internal absolute imports (@/)
import { neofloTheme } from '@/src/theme';
import { tokens } from '@/src/tokens';

// 4. Relative imports
import { ButtonProps } from './Button.types';

// 5. Type-only imports (last group, marked with `type`)
import type { ReactNode } from 'react';
```

### No deep MUI imports

```tsx
// ❌ Wrong (breaks in MUI v7+)
import createTheme from '@mui/material/styles/createTheme';

// ✅ Right
import { createTheme } from '@mui/material/styles';
```

### Use named imports

```tsx
// ❌ Avoid default exports for components (except Next.js pages)
export default function Button() { /* ... */ }

// ✅ Use named exports
export function Button() { /* ... */ }
export { Button };
```

Exception: Next.js `page.tsx`, `layout.tsx`, `route.ts` files require default exports.

## React patterns

### Functional components only

No class components anywhere.

### Always use `React.forwardRef` for wrapper components

If the wrapped MUI component accepts a ref, the wrapper must forward it:

```tsx
// ✅ Right
export const Button = React.forwardRef<HTMLButtonElement, ButtonProps>(
  ({ variant = 'primary', ...props }, ref) => (
    <MuiButton ref={ref} {...variantMap[variant]} {...props} />
  )
);
Button.displayName = 'Button';
```

### Always set `displayName`

For all components (including those using `forwardRef`):

```tsx
Button.displayName = 'Button';
```

### Props destructuring

Destructure props in the function signature, not the body:

```tsx
// ❌ Avoid
function Button(props: ButtonProps) {
  const variant = props.variant ?? 'primary';
  // ...
}

// ✅ Prefer
function Button({ variant = 'primary', size = 'md', ...rest }: ButtonProps) {
  // ...
}
```

### `'use client'` directive

Required at the top of any file using:
- MUI components (most of them)
- React hooks (`useState`, `useEffect`, etc.)
- Event handlers
- Browser APIs

```tsx
'use client';

import { Button as MuiButton } from '@mui/material';
// ...
```

## Comments

### When to comment

- Explain **why**, not what
- Document non-obvious business rules
- Explain workarounds for library bugs (link to issue)
- Mark `TODO` with a tracking ticket: `// TODO(ATOMS-123): fix race condition`

### When NOT to comment

```tsx
// ❌ Useless — code is self-explanatory
// Increment counter
counter++;

// ❌ Stale comments — code says one thing, comment says another
// Returns user name
function getUserId() { return user.id; }
```

### JSDoc on public APIs

Every exported component, function, and type must have JSDoc:

```tsx
/**
 * Primary action component for clickable actions.
 *
 * @example
 * <Button variant="primary">Submit</Button>
 */
export const Button = (...) => { /* ... */ };
```

## Error handling

### Throw `Error` objects, not strings

```tsx
// ❌ Wrong
throw 'Component not found';

// ✅ Right
throw new Error('Component not found');
```

### Use Error subclasses for known error types

```tsx
class ComponentNotFoundError extends Error {
  constructor(name: string) {
    super(`Component "${name}" not found`);
    this.name = 'ComponentNotFoundError';
  }
}

throw new ComponentNotFoundError(componentName);
```

### Never silently swallow errors

```tsx
// ❌ Wrong
try {
  await doSomething();
} catch {}

// ✅ Right
try {
  await doSomething();
} catch (error) {
  console.error('Failed to do something:', error);
  throw error; // or handle it meaningfully
}
```

## Async code

### Always use `async`/`await`, never raw `.then()`

```tsx
// ❌ Avoid
function loadData() {
  return fetch('/api').then(r => r.json()).then(data => process(data));
}

// ✅ Prefer
async function loadData() {
  const response = await fetch('/api');
  const data = await response.json();
  return process(data);
}
```

### Mark async functions with explicit return types

```tsx
async function getComponent(name: string): Promise<ComponentData | null> {
  // ...
}
```

## File organization

### One component per file

Each file exports one main component. Helper components used only by that main component can live in the same file but should not be exported.

### File size limit

If a file exceeds **300 lines**, split it. Components, types, examples, and styled-elements should be in separate files.

## Console statements

### Strip console.log before merging

`console.log` is not allowed in committed code. Use during development, then remove.

`console.error` is allowed for genuine error reporting.
`console.warn` is allowed for deprecation warnings or non-critical issues.

```tsx
// ❌ Wrong (in committed code)
console.log('User clicked button');

// ✅ Right
console.error('Failed to load component data:', error);
console.warn('Button prop "color" is deprecated. Use "variant" instead.');
```

## ESLint compliance

The repo uses `eslint-config-next` with strict rules. Run `npm run lint` before pushing. Do not disable ESLint rules with `// eslint-disable-next-line` unless absolutely necessary, and always include a reason:

```tsx
// ❌ Wrong
// eslint-disable-next-line
const x = anyValue;

// ✅ Right (if truly necessary)
// eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any -- Required for third-party API typing
const x: any = thirdPartyApi.getData();
```

## Git commit messages

Follow Conventional Commits format:

```
<type>(<scope>): <subject>

<optional body>
```

Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `test`, `chore`

Examples:
```
feat(button): add destructive variant
fix(textfield): correct error state border color
docs(readme): update installation steps
refactor(theme): extract palette into separate file
chore(deps): bump @mui/material to 9.0.2
```

## Pre-merge checklist

Before every PR is ready to merge:

- [ ] No emojis in any code file
- [ ] No `any` types added
- [ ] All exported functions have explicit return types
- [ ] All components have `displayName` set
- [ ] All `'use client'` directives are in place for interactive components
- [ ] No `console.log` statements
- [ ] `npm run lint` passes
- [ ] `npm run generate` was run if components/tokens changed
- [ ] JSDoc added to new exported APIs
